/target
# Built bundles, not fixtures. A `.velac` under `tests/golden/` is a golden: it is
# committed precisely so that the loader can be tested against a container it did not
# just write, and so that a format change has something to fail against.
**/*.velac
!tests/golden/**/*.velac

# `tools/drive.sh` writes here when `--out` is not given. A capture is an artifact, not a
# source file: one was committed by a `git add -A` before this rule existed.
/frame.png

# A build's output. `vela build` writes here by default, and one being committed by hand is
# the same mistake `/frame.png` was — a generated file that reads like a source file.
**/dist/

# A player's saves, written beside the project they belong to. Player data, not source — and
# one dropped into an example by trying it out is exactly the file a `git add -A` commits.
#
# `tests/golden/saves/` is the deliberate exception: that one *is* a fixture, and the tests
# read it. Negating it here works because its parent is not itself excluded — which is why the
# pattern is anchored at the directory rather than at the files inside it.
**/saves/
!tests/golden/saves/

# A migrated Ren'Py project: the output of `vela migrate`, kept so there is a real project to try
# the engine against. The migration *tool* is source; what it produces is an artifact — it is
# regenerated by running the command — and it carries someone else's art, so it is not committed.
/examples/the_question_migrated/
